--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` throws for a `#[Command]` parameter no argument's text is converted into yet, rather than guessing — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6 admits a *subset* of an enum's cases among the types an argument may be
// given at, and this runtime does not convert one yet: `nvs_runtime::commands`'s
// gap 1 is that subset, and nothing else — the whole enum beside it converts by
// its case name (`command-run-converts-an-enum-argument-by-its-case-name`), so
// what is left is a filter over those same pairs rather than an open question.
// The two wrong answers are a plausible first case and a usage error blaming
// the command line, so it is neither: a `LogicError`, which is what a mistake
// in the *program* is, since the table is fixed at compile time and no input
// can change what this row declares.
enum Level {
    Quiet,
    Loud,
}

class Radio {
    #[Core\Command(name: "tune")]
    public static function tune(Level::Quiet|Level::Loud $level): uint {
        echo "tune ran\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

try {
    uint $status = Core\Command::run();
    echo "status: ", $status, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--ARGS--
tune
Loud
--EXPECT--
`Core\Command::run` cannot convert an argument into the type `tune`'s `level` is declared at yet
